m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Histidine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: The network centers on the only demonstrated prebiotic synthesis: surface aqueous-phase condensation of erythrose (from the formose reaction) with formamidine (from HCN/NH3), ring closure to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde, and Strecker-type cyanation/hydrolysis to histidine\u2014a contested, low-yield (3.5%) pathway shown experimentally by Shen, Yang, Miller & Or\u00f3 (1987, 1990). This is complemented by literature-derived and speculative branches from imidazole derivatives and semi-enzymatic biotic scenarios. Ten distinct pathways are explicitly represented: (1) canonical sugar+amidine route; (2\u20133) via imidazole-4-glycol/ethanol side products; (4) from prebiotic HCN-polymer hydrolysates (trace, unconfirmed); (5) speculative sequential Strecker from imidazole-4-acetaldehyde; (6) semi-enzymatic HisF/IGP-like assembly; (7\u20138) conversion of imidazole-4-carboxylic/pyruvic acid intermediates (hypothetical hydrothermal/meteoritic); (9) Debus condensation (wrong regioisomer, side-branch); (10) C2\u2013C2 coupling (cyanosulfidic, speculative). The bottleneck is standing up the C4 sugar+amidine condensation; all mineral and environmental assignments are justified per the evidence, with contested and hypothetical links 