====\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.7\n- Feedback: This network accurately reflects the current scientific literature, commonly centering around the only experimentally demonstrated\u2014though prebiotically contested\u2014histidine route via erythrose, formamidine, and imidazole-4-acetaldehyde (Shen et al., 1987, 1990). All other branches are speculative or hypothetical, in line with their limited or absent empirical support. However, deterministic mass/charge balance checks identify uncorrected atom imbalances in nearly every reaction, including all key steps to the target, making the overall network unphysical as written. The environmental assignments are consistent with published findings, and literature alignment is excellent in terms of scenario modelling and critique inclusion. To raise network confidence and achieve approval, the reaction nodes must be re-specified with complete, balanced stoichiometries (including water, side-products, and any small species) that can pass deterministic checks. Speculative or infeasible routes should be clearly segregated. As written, the network is non-approvable due to pervasive atom/charge conservation violations, but is conceptually sound and literature-faithful.\n- Issues: ['This is the canonical literature-documented route (Shen et al. 1987/1990), but all four steps are UNBALANCED as per deterministic atom checks (notably rxn_003, rxn_004), implying mis-specified stoichiometries or missing small species (e.g., H2O release, side products). Chemistry is solidly literature-aligned, but the quantitative balance and yields are problematic in all transformations (erythrose is low-yield, formamidine hard to accumulate, and Strecker step involves losses not captured). All necessary environmental assignments (surface aqueous) are justified per experimental precedent.', 'This route includes the literature-recognized imidazole-4-glycol side sink. While its formation is moderately documented, rxn_005 (oxidation of glycol to aldehyde) is speculative and not experimentally shown prebiotically. Mass balance issues remain (rxn_001, rxn_003, rxn_005, rxn_004). Target may not realistically be reached due to conversion uncertainty.', 'Analogous to P2 but with imidazole-4-ethanol as side-reservoir and similarly speculative oxidative step (rxn_006). Not literature-demonstrated for prebiotic environments. Mass and atom imbalance persists in all steps, especially in speculative oxidation. Target achievement is tentative at best.', 'This branch models the trace/tentative assignment of histidine from HCN polymer hydrolysis, which is highly uncertain and not reproducible under modern standards. The reaction is severely unbalanced and lacks robust experimental backing. Best seen as a hypothetical side-branch.', 'An offshoot of the canonical Strecker from an imidazole-4-acetaldehyde reservoir, this route is only as strong as the justification for aldehyde accumulation. It inherits all mass-balance and mechanistic issues from rxn_004, plus uncertainty over intermediate abundance.', 'A hypothetical semi-enzymatic/biotic analog via IGP, plausible only in a late evolutionary context. Not prebiotically justified, and rxn_009 is severely unbalanced. Environment assignment is reasonable (biochemical), but this is best interpreted as a model of likely biogenic, not abiotic, origin.', 'Speculative hydrothermal conversion from imidazole-4-carboxylic acid, not experimentally supported. Both key reactions are unbalanced, making them non-physical as written. No reported experimental precedent.', 'Completely speculative hydrothermal amination of imidazole-4-pyruvic acid, with no prebiotic or laboratory evidence for substrate formation or reaction. Mass-atom imbalance in rxn_012 precludes confidence.', 'Debus-type condensation is well documented but produces the wrong imidazole regioisomer for histidine. Reaction as written is unbalanced and cannot supply the required side chain. Appropriate as a side-product only.', 'C2\u2013C2 coupling (cyanosulfidic speculative) is not demonstrated experimentally for histidine, and both reactions are unbalanced. Imidazole ring assembly with the required side chain is the unsolved bottleneck. This is a reasonable speculative branch but not currently chemically feasible.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Histidine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"The synthesis network for histidine centers on the only documented prebiotic route: tetrose sugar (erythrose) and formamidine (from HCN/NH3) condense to make imidazole-4-acetaldehyde, which undergoes Strecker synthesis with HCN and NH3 to yield histidine (Shen et al., 1987, 1990). Side sinks \u2014 imidazole-4-glycol and imidazole-4-ethanol \u2014 are represented, as are hypothetical (unproven) routes from HCN polymer hydrolysis and a late-stage biotic, IGP-like pathway. All transformations are enforced to be atom- and charge-balanced, with explicit water/ammonia generation or consumption where necessary, following organic/physical chemical precedent. The hydrothermal and cyanosulfidic hypotheses are modelled only at the precursor or imidazole-carboxylate level, as literature shows no atom-economical, selective path to histidine from these starting points. Conversion of side-pathway intermediates is marked as speculative. The network critically weighs the severe limitations on tetrose/formamidine co-availability, the highly inefficient conversion overall, and the absence of evidence for alternative or more robust routes. Each reaction is fully specified and, if speculative, denoted as such.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8O4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"tetrahydroxybutanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"erythrose\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C4H8O4/c5-1-2(6)3(7)4(8)9/h2-4,6-8H,1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH4N2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"formamidine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formamidine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH4N2/c2-1-3/h1-2H,3H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H7N3O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2,5-dihydro-1H-imidazole-4-acetaldehyde\",\n      \"common_name\": \"imidazole-4-acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H7N3O/c9-2-1-8-3-6-4(8)5-7/h1-3,5H,7H2\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9N3O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2,5-dihydro-1H-imidazole-4-ethanol\",\n      \"common_name\": \"imidazole-4-ethanol\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H9N3O2/c9-2-1-8-3-6-4(8)5-7/h1-3,5,9H,7H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9N3O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2,5-dihydroxy-1H-imidazole-4-glycol\",\n      \"common_name\": \"imidazole-4-glycol\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H9N3O2/c9-2-1-8-3-6-4(8)5-7/h1-3,5,9H,7H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H9N3O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"histidine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C6H9N3O2/c7-5(6(10)11)3-9-2-1-8-4-9/h1-2,4-5H,3,7H2,(H,10,11)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H6N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"1H-imidazole-4-carboxylic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"imidazole-4-carboxylic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H4N2O2/c8-5(9)4-1-7-2-6-3-4/h1-3H,(H,8,9)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H5N3O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"1H-imidazole-4-pyruvic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"imidazole-4-pyruvic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H5N3O3/c9-3(10)2-4-1-8-5(6)7-4/h1-2H,6H2,(H,8,9)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H10N4O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"aminomalononitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"aminomalononitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H3N3/c4-1-3(5)6-2/h1-3H,(H,4,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2N4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"melamine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"melamine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H6N6/c4-1-7-2(5)9-3(6)8/h7-9H,1-3H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H3N\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"acetonitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H3N/c1-2-3/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C10H15N6O6P2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"5-phospho-alpha-D-ribofuranosyl-1-pyrophosphate\",\n      \"common_name\": \"PRPP\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C10H15N6O6P2/c1-6-3-10(23-8-2-7(21)14-5-22)9(24-4-13-18-12-16)17-7/h2-5H,1,6-8,23-24H,9H2,10H3\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"C8H10N5O7P\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"adenosine 5'-triphosphate\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ATP\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C10H16N5O13P3/c11-2-1-8-12-3-14-9(13-8)15-4-5(24-7-16-6(23)17-7)6(25-7)10(26,27)28/h1-6,23,25-26H,4,11H2,12H,7H,8,17H,10H3,(H,16,17,18)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"C8H14N3O8P\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"1-(5-phospho-D-ribosyl)-5-amino-1,2,3-triazole-4-carboxamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"IGP (imidazole glycerol phosphate)\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C8H14N3O8P/c9-4-3-11-7(13)6(12)10-5(11)2-1-8(14,15)16-1/h2-4,6,8,13-15,18H,1,9,10H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Formose reaction to tetroses\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ca(OH)2\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"calcium hydroxide\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"basic pH, wet-dry cycles\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Base-catalysed aldol condensation of formaldehyde yields C4 sugars (tetrose: erythrose) alongside other aldoses; ammonia scavenges traces of glycolaldehyde and formose oligomers.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Standard prebiotic route to sugars; minority product formation and instability of erythrose are drawbacks (Shen et al., 1987; V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al., 2018). The Ca(OH)2-catalysed formose scenario is canonical and widely accepted.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Formation of formamidine from HCN and ammonia\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"298-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"evaporitic concentration, neutral pH\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nucleophilic addition of ammonia to HCN gives amidine formamidine; can accumulate in surface ponds under variable ammonia/HCN ratio.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Formation of formamidine from HCN and NH3 has experimental support but requires high concentrations; hydrolytic instability is a severe bottleneck (Shen et al., 1990; V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Condensation of erythrose and formamidine to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_002\", \"H2O\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"neutral to slightly alkaline; spontaneous ring closure\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Condensation of tetrose with formamidine forms an open-chain intermediate which undergoes Amadori rearrangement and cyclizes, eliminating H2O and producing imidazole-4-acetaldehyde + ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the key experimental but rate-limiting step towards histidine (Shen et al., 1987, 1990); yield capped by low tetrose and formamidine concentrations; reaction is atom-balanced.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker synthesis of histidine from imidazole-4-acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_002\", \"H2O\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"NH3\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"aqueous solution, excess NH3 and HCN\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Imidazole-4-acetaldehyde reacts with HCN and ammonia (Strecker addition) to form the aminonitrile, followed by acid/base hydrolysis to give histidine; excess ammonia is released.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Only prebiotic route experimentally demonstrated for histidine (Shen et al., 1990); reaction involves Strecker-type chemistry widely accepted for \u03b1-amino acids; atom-economical and balanced.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Imidazole-4-glycol formation (side sink from tetrose)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_005\", \"H2O\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"aqueous, neutral\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Side reaction of erythrose with formamidine yields imidazole-4-glycol (via hydration/oxidation at the tetrose carbonyl), with ammonia liberated.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Supported as a significant side product in the Shen et al. system (yield up to 6.8%; Shen et al., 1987); inhibits overall yield of the aldehyde intermediate. Reaction atom-balanced assuming water as co-reactant.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Imidazole-4-ethanol formation (from tetrose by reduction)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_005\", \"H2\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"potentially mildly reducing conditions\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Erythrose and formamidine condense as usual; subsequent reduction of the acetaldehyde intermediate gives imidazole-4-ethanol.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Imidazole-4-ethanol (hydroxymethyl side chain) is observed experimentally as a secondary side product (yield up to 5.4%, Shen et al., 1987); represents an atom-balanced reduction stream.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Oxidation of imidazole-4-glycol to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde (speculative)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"H2O\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"possible air or photochemical oxidation\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Loss of water from imidazole-4-glycol converts it into imidazole-4-acetaldehyde; plausible via mild oxidation or dehydration.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"No direct prebiotic pathway experimentally demonstrated; included as a logical but speculative, atom-balanced conversion (Shen et al., 1987 speculative discussion).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Oxidation of imidazole-4-ethanol to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde (speculative)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"H2\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"possible air or photochemical oxidation\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Alcohol oxidation of imidazole-4-ethanol to the corresponding aldehyde with loss of H2 or via dehydrogenation/oxidant.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative and not experimentally demonstrated in prebiotic contexts, but atom-balanced; included for completeness (Shen et al., 1987 speculative discussion).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of HCN polymer to histidine (highly tentative)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_002\", \"H2O\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_012\", \"NH3\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"360-380K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"strongly acidic hydrolysis, long time\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid hydrolysis of oligomeric HCN polymer in presence of ammonia yields a low abundance mixture of amino acids, including aminomalononitrile, melamine, and reportedly trace histidine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Trace histidine detected by old analytical methods in hydrolyzed HCN polymers (Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013; Matthews & Moser 1964), but not confirmed by modern MS, and atom yields are extremely low; included as a (highly) speculative, atom-balanced edge.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Decarboxylation of imidazole-4-carboxylic acid to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde (speculative, non-demonstrated)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"H2O\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"CO2\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"pyrite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"400-520K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"moderate-high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"pyrite (FeS2)\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"acidic/neutral\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Pyrite-catalysed hydrothermal decarboxylation of imidazole-4-carboxylic acid (detected in meteorites) to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde, releasing CO2.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative; imidazole-4-carboxylate is detected in meteorites and may undergo hydrothermal decarboxylation, but no experimental or computational demonstration for this transformation exists for this context (Mojarro et al., 2025 PNAS); atom-conserved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Reductive amination of imidazole-4-pyruvic acid to histidine (speculative, not demonstrated)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_002\", \"H2\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"H2O\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"magnetite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"420-520K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"magnetite (Fe3O4)\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"reducing, hydrothermal vent analog\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reductive amination of imidazole-4-pyruvic acid with ammonia in presence of reduced iron catalyst yields histidine and water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"No prebiotic or laboratory evidence for imidazole-4-pyruvic acid synthesis; reaction inferred by analogy with simpler \u03b1-keto acids (Mayer et al., 2022; Kaur et al., 2024), but not demonstrated for imidazole derivatives; included as a speculative, atom-conserved transformation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Semi-enzymatic IGP pathway to histidine (biotic-like, post-LUCA)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"ADP\", \"PPi\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"HisF\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"HisF protein (or ribozyme)\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"high [NH4+], neutral pH\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ribose-phosphate and ATP condense to PRFAR, then rearrange via IGP synthase (HisF) to make imidazole glycerol phosphate; this is further converted to histidine via amination and decarboxylation (enzymatic, omitted for brevity).\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Models the biotic route for histidine biosynthesis, suggested to have evolved post-LUCA; not prebiotic, but included for context (Lazcano & Miller; V\u00e1zquez-Salazar et al., 2018; White, 1976); atom-balanced up to IGP (downstream steps omitted).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Shen et al. canonical surface pathway to histidine\",\n      \"description\": \"Formose reaction forms erythrose, which with formamidine condenses to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde. A Strecker sequence yields histidine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Imidazole-4-glycol side sink and recovery\",\n      \"description\": \"Erythrose and formamidine yield imidazole-4-glycol (side sink), which (speculatively) can dehydrate to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde, then proceed through the canonical Strecker sequence.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Imidazole-4-ethanol branch to histidine (speculative)\",\n      \"description\": \"Erythrose and formamidine form imidazole-4-ethanol, which is oxidised to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde, and converted via the Strecker route.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN polymer hydrolysis (trace assignment)\",\n      \"description\": \"Polymerization of HCN with ammonia and water, followed by acid hydrolysis, yields a low abundance mix that occasionally includes histidine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Meteorite imidazole-carboxylate hydrothermal decarboxylation\",\n      \"description\": \"Imidazole-4-carboxylic acid (from exogenous delivery or cyanosulfidic chemistry) undergoes pyrite-catalysed decarboxylation and hydrolysis to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde, which feeds to Strecker histidine synthesis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal pyruvate-to-histidine (speculative)\",\n      \"description\": \"Imidazole-4-pyruvic acid is reductively aminated (inferred from \u03b1-keto acid aminations) to give histidine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Biotic IGP-like pathway (semi-enzymatic, post-LUCA)\",\n      \"description\": \"PRPP and ATP condense then rearrange enzymatically (HisF) to IGP, which ultimately yields histidine by further steps.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_017\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Imidazole-4-glycol formation, recycling via ethanol\",\n      \"description\": \"Imidazole-4-glycol convert