e via mild oxidation or dehydration.", "reasoning": "No direct prebiotic pathway experimentally demonstrated; included as a logical but speculative, atom-balanced conversion (Shen et al., 1987 speculative discussion)."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Oxidation of imidazole-4-ethanol to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde (speculative)", "inputs": ["mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "H2"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [null], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "possible air or photochemical oxidation"}, "mechanism": "Alcohol oxidation of imidazole-4-ethanol to the corresponding aldehyde with loss of H2 or via dehydrogenation/oxidant.", "reasoning": "Speculative and not experimentally demonstrated in prebiotic contexts, but atom-balanced; included for completeness (Shen et al., 1987 speculative discussion)."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Hydrolysis of HCN polymer to histidine (highly tentative)", "inputs": ["mol_003", "mol_002", "H2O"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_012", "NH3"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [null], "conditions": {"temperature": "360-380K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "strongly acidic hydrolysis, long time"}, "mechanism": "Acid hydrolysis of oligomeric HCN polymer in presence of ammonia yields a low abundance mixture of amino acids, including aminomalononitrile, melamine, and reportedly trace histidine.", "reasoning": "Trace histidine detected by old analytical methods in hydrolyzed HCN polymers (Ruiz-Bermejo et al., 2013; Matthews & Moser 1964), but not confirmed by modern MS, and atom yields are extremely low; included as a (highly) speculative, atom-balanced edge."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Decarboxylation of imidazole-4-carboxylic acid to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde (speculative, non-demonstrated)", "inputs": ["mol_010", "H2O"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "CO2"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["pyrite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "4